/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  Component: Nitrogen  (N2, CAS 7727-37-9)  --  the PERMANENT GAS.

  The INERT.  Its job in this case is to guarantee that a vapour phase
  exists at every condition swept, so that "no vapour formed" can never be
  confused with "the run failed".

  INERT DOES NOT MEAN INSOLUBLE.  N2 dissolves, and its dissolution is
  priced like any other (chemistry/N2-dissolution.dat).  What it does not do
  is IONISE -- and that is exactly what `aqueousSpeciation none;` says.  The
  fact is about the speciation NETWORK, not about solubility: a component
  can be `none` and still have a Henry rung.  Conflating the two is the
  mistake this line exists to prevent.

  AND `noncondensable true;` IS THE OTHER HALF OF IT.  `aqueousSpeciation`
  says what nitrogen does in the CHEMISTRY; `noncondensable` says what it does
  in the LIQUID, and for a permanent gas the two point different ways.  The
  molecular backbone prices a liquid mixture on the pure-component
  (Lewis-Randall / Raoult) reference, and a species above its critical
  temperature has no pure liquid to reference: the Antoine block below stops
  at 126 K and this case runs at 308.  Read there, nitrogen's partial pressure
  is a number off the end of a curve.

  So the engine takes it OFF the backbone and onto the HENRY rung, through
  chemistry/N2-dissolution.dat -- and its dissolved amount becomes an aqueous
  total like any other family's.  That bridge is NOT declared here, and must
  not be: the gas-liquid record's `dissolvedSpecies` is already the one
  curated home for which species is dissolved nitrogen, and writing it a
  second time makes `aqueousSpeciation none;` a lie (check_resolver_coherence
  refuses it in those words).

  Values mirrored from data/standards/components/N2.dat.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        N2;
formula     N2;
CAS         7727-37-9;
noncondensable true;

MW          28.013;
Tc          126.20;
Pc          33.98;
omega       0.039;
Tb          77.35;
HvapTb      5577;
Vliq        3.46e-5;

standardThermochemistry
{
    dHf_298   0.0;              // J/mol  -- element in its standard state
    s_298     191.610;          // J/(mol*K)  -- third-law absolute
}

vaporPressure
{
    model         Antoine;
    coefficients  (3.7362   264.651   -6.788);
    Trange        (50  126);
}

idealGasHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (31.15   -1.357e-2   2.680e-5   -1.168e-8);
    Trange        (250  1500);
}

// Physically extrapolated stub: N2 is a gas at every condition of this case.
liquidHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (56.0);
}

aqueousSpeciation  none;
